            <ArticleTitle>Age related clinical features of childhood coeliac disease in Australia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To describe the presenting clinical features of coeliac disease in a single paediatric centre, and to determine if the presenting features vary with age.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A review was conducted of children who had been referred with clinical suspicion of coeliac disease to the paediatric gastroenterology department of a tertiary paediatric hospital in Sydney, Australia. Coeliac disease was defined using standard histological criteria. Medical records were reviewed retrospectively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Clinical data were available for 74 cases of proven coeliac disease. Only 9% of patients were less than 2 years of age at diagnosis. Pre-school children (age &lt; 5 years) presented with different symptoms to school children (age &gt; or = 5 years). The most common presenting features in younger children were diarrhoea, irritability and weight loss. However, in older children, abdominal pain was the most common presenting feature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found a significant difference in the clinical features of coeliac disease in pre-school compared to school age children.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
